  - .assets/img-103-04-05.png
  - .assets/img-103-01-02.png

steps:
  - cmds:
      - view 1
    text: |
      {[ht,$start]&thinsp;Elevating Register Access}

      Every **EMS** distro eventually delivers target-specific drivers dependent upon memory-mapped MCU registers.

      Each distro therefore bundles vendor-supplied headers {[dc,1]}, forming a "source-of-truth" that defines all MCU registers.

      To streamline register access, every distro contains a spec&shy;ial script {[dc,2]} which _generates_ a matching **EMS** facade {[dc,3]}.

      Named {[cu]REGS}, drivers will conventionally {[ck]import} the gen&shy;erated facade under a local {[cs]$R} alias.

      <div class="em-info"

      Guided by **IntelliSense**, clients can easily form {[cs]$R} selec&shy;tion expressions using vendor-defined register names.

      </div>

      The **EMS** translator {[dc,4]} then _lowers_ {[cs]$R} ex&shy;pressions to tar&shy;get C++ code that uses the original register headers {[dc,1]}.

  - cmds:
      - view 2
    text: |
      {[ht,arrow_circle_right]&thinsp;Register Access &ndash; Vendor Headers}

      When bringing up a new **EMS** distro, toggling a GPIO pin connected to an LED often serves an initial "smoke test".

      <div class="em-info">

      We'll stay with {[em]Board &ndash; &lt;bare-metal&gt;}, but will switch to {[em]Setup &ndash; nordic.nrf52://default} {[dc,1]} as our new target.

      </div>

      The {[cu]GpioNatG} program {[dc,2]} configures {[cn]APP_LED_PIN} as an output GPIO and then lowers its voltage.

      When translating {[cu]GpioNatG}{[dc,3]}, **EMS** retains all {[ct]e$}{[cs]&#96;..&#96;} code snippets within the generated `GpioNatG.cpp` file {[dc,4]}.

      Adding just two instructions to setup and toggle the LED, the {[cu]GpioNatG} programs weighs in at only 168 bytes {[dc,5]}.

  - cmds:
      - view 3
    text: |
      {[ht,arrows_left_right_circle]&thinsp;Register Access &ndash; Headers vs Facade}

      To appreciate the {[cu]REGS} facade, let's compare {[cu]GpioNatG} with an alternative {[cu]GpioRegG} program {[dc,2]} that uses {[cs]$R}.

      <div class="em-info">

      {[cs]$R} offers syntactic _and_ semantic sugar &ndash; blending nicely with the **EMS** language services supported by **VS Code**.

      </div>

      But here, the **EMS** translator {[dc,3]} lowers {[cs]$R} expres&shy;sions to C++ equivalents in {[dc,4]} which directly use header symbols.

      Though a bit more stylzed, compilation of {[dc,4]} within the {[cu]GpioRegG} program yields an _identical_ binary image {[dc,5]}.

  - cmds:
      - open 4
      - mark 4 A 4
      - mark 4 B 15
      - mark 4 C 19
      - mark 4 D 23
    focus: [4, 1]
    acts: ["$reveal"]
    text: |
      {[ht,$details]&thinsp;%[cu]UartRegG% &ndash; Transmitting Bytes}

      Like toggling a GPIO, transmitting single bytes over a UART represents an early milestone in supporting any MCU.

      Still working {[em]&lt;bare-metal&gt;} and targeting {[em]nordic.nrf52}, the {[cu]UartRegG} program performs basic output on {[cn]UART0}.

      After declaring {[cs]$R} as the local {[cu]Regs} alias {[bm,A]}, {[cu]UartRegG} initializes {[cn]UART0} and then output a pair of characters.

      {[cs]$R} selections can appear on the right-hand side of as&shy;sign&shy;ments&thinsp;{[bm,B]} &ndash; choosing a vendor-defined symbolic constant.

      <div class="em-info">

      Note the **EMS** syntax {[bm,C]} for denoting character literals.

      </div>

      Finally, the {[cf]put} function {[bm,D]} outputs a single 8-bit value and then polls {[cn]UART0} status flags for completion.

  - cmds:
      - view 5
    text: |
      {[ht,sync_arrow_down]&thinsp;Lowering %[cs]$R% Expressions}

      **EMS** translates {[cs]$R} expressions into equivalent C++ forms which use definitions from vendor-supplied headers.

      To guide {[cs]$R} translation, **EMS** relies upon *build property* bind&shy;ings typically found in some distro `setup-*.ini` file:

      {[DC,1]}{[cn]ModFmt} matches names of MCU peripherals<br>
      {[DC,2]}{[cn]SelFmt} matches names of peripheral registers<br>
      {[DC,3]}{[cn]FldFmt} matches value names of register fields<br>

      Format strings bound to these properties specify a corres&shy;ponding C++ fragment to inject upon matching {[cs]$R} terms.

      <div class="em-info">

      The **EMS** *dereference* operator [{[ct]$$}] maps to a C++ help&shy;er [{[ct]em}::{[cf]$reg32}] defined in `emscript.hpp`&thinsp;{[tr,103/03/04]}.

      </div>

      The next stop explains the supporting role of `REGS.hpp`.

  - cmds:
      - open 6
      - fold 6 9 189
      - fold 6 190 220
      - fold 6 228 2897
      - fold 6 2901 2933
      - mark 6 A 2
      - mark 6 B 5
      - mark 6 C 224
      - mark 6 D 2899
      - mark 6 E 2945
    focus: [6, 1]
    acts: ["$reveal"]
    text: |
      {[ht,sync_arrow_up]&thinsp;The %[cu]REGS% Facade}

      While generated by a distro-specific script, key aspects of the {[cu]REGS} facade design deserve our attention.

      For one, the facade only exists in the build-time meta do&shy;main &ndash; hence {[cu]REGS} declares its role as {[cs]'COMPOSITE'}{[bm,A]}.

      Like other composites imported through {[cu]BuildC}&thinsp;{[tr,103/03/04]}, {[cu]REGS} generates a C++ file {[bm,B]} which `main.cpp` includes.

      <div class="em-info">

      The prior stop showed how `REGS.hpp` brings in vendor-supplied headers defining names used in {[cs]$R} selections.

      </div>

      The remainder of {[cu]REGS} follows a predictable pattern:

      {[BM,C]}peripherals define types that aggregate their registers<br>
      {[BM,D]}constants define register-specific symbolic values<br>
      {[BM,E]}constants declare instances of peripheral types<br>

  - cmds:
      - open 7
      - fold 7 5 12
      - fold 7 17 88
      - fold 7 104 116
      - mark 7 A 14
      - mark 7 B 90
      - mark 7 C 100
      - mark 7 D 104
      - mark 7 E 118
      - mark 7 F 121
      - mark 7 G 124
    focus: [7, 1]
    text: |
      {[ht,$peek]&thinsp;<code>genregs.ts</code>}

      Each distro maintains a `genregs.ts` script which synthe&shy;sizes its {[cu]REGS} facade by parsing vendor-supplied headers.

      Specialized per distro and shaped by header file organi&shy;zation, this [**TypeScript**] code follows a common design:

      {[BM,A]}a {[ct]Map} declaring periperal instance&thinsp;&middot;&thinsp;type names<br>
      {[BM,B]}the prologue of the generated {[cs]'REGS.em.ts'} file<br>
      {[BM,C]}the {[cs]'inc/nrf52.h'} header text, split line-by-line<br>
      {[BM,D]}a helper function to output peripheral&thinsp;&middot;&thinsp;register types<br>
      {[BM,E]}the lines of the {[cs]'inc/nrf52_bitfields.h'} header<br>
      {[BM,F]}a helper to output per-register symbolic values<br>
      {[BM,G]}output of declarations tracking {[cn]TYPE_MAP}<br>

        💡&thinsp;In practice, you won't need to modify `genreg.ts`.<br>
        ❓&thinsp;Otherwise, seek help at **EM&bull;porium** [Discussions](https://github.com/em-foundation/emporium/discussions).

  - cmds:
      - view 8
    text: |
      {[ht,$extra]&thinsp;Extra Credit}

      Using the `nordic.nrf52` distro as a point of reference:

      {[DC,1]} Compare content across the `*.distro.*` buckets

      {[DC,2]} Explore project output after building these gists.

      {[DC,3]} Trace the impact of `setup-sram` when building.

  - cmds:
      - view 9
    text: |
      {[ht,$steps]&thinsp;Next Steps}

      Good news&thinsp;&hellip;&thinsp; We've reached the midpoint of the **Distro Packages** tours, having thoroughly explored {[cu]BuildC}.

      Better news&thinsp;&hellip;&thinsp; You'll find the shift in focus from {[cu]BuildC} to {[cu]BoardC} going forward much easier to grasp.

      {[hc]}
